A function compiled with `-fstack-protector-strong` contains:

```c
struct user {
    char name[16];
    int  is_admin;
} u;
u.is_admin = 0;
strcpy(u.name, input);
if (u.is_admin) grant();
```

An input of 20 bytes with non-zero bytes at positions 16 to 19 is passed. What happens?

findings.correction:
Consider this deliberately invalid copy in a function using stack protection. Assume a conventional ABI with `is_admin` at offset 16 and a four-byte int. Why is a canary not enough to guarantee the authorization decision is safe? The C overflow has undefined behaviour; no outcome is guaranteed.

```c
struct user {
    char name[16];
    int  is_admin;
} u;
u.is_admin = 0;
